{# The application's inline stroke icons. One macro keyed by name so callers stay
   terse: {{ icons.icon('movies') }}, or {{ icons.icon('logout', 20) }} where a
   caller needs a different size.

   Every glyph shares one drawing style — a 24-unit viewBox, no fill, a
   currentColor stroke at 1.7, round caps and joins — so a new one can be dropped
   in without looking foreign. Callers:

     category tabs + sort   all, movies, tv-shows, tv-seasons, collections, sort,
                            sort-title, sort-date, sort-direction
     header nav + tray      wall, import, orphans, support, logout
     poster actions         change, send, fetch, download, related, fullscreen,
                            delete

   `fetch` is deliberately an alias of `import` rather than a second drawing of
   the same idea — see the alias table below. #}

{# The import glyph is sideways rather than downward: an arrow falling onto a
   tray is the download idiom, and importing is not downloading. Turned a quarter
   anticlockwise it becomes an arrow entering a boundary, where the boundary is
   Marquee. The geometry is that older path rotated exactly, so the 6px gap
   between the arrowhead and the bar is the one it always had.

   `send` is that same glyph with the arrowhead moved to the far end of an
   identical shaft. Shaft and bar do not move, so direction is the only thing
   separating Send to Plex from Fetch from Plex — the two actions that move one
   image opposite ways and cannot be undone. Note that the downward arrow the
   import glyph gave up is exactly right for `download`, which now uses it. #}

{# `related` is three poster cards in a row — a full-height one in the centre with
   a shorter sibling either side. The centre is the poster the action was started
   from and the flanking pair is the rest of its set, which is the whole idea of
   Related posters. Drawn as cards rather than as a folder so it cannot be read as
   the Collections category, and as a row rather than a grid so it cannot be read
   as the All tab. It replaced the `copy` glyph in place when Copy URL left the
   action stack; no caller of `copy` remained. #}

{# The sort control's three marks divide the work: `sort-title` and `sort-date`
   name the field, `sort-direction` states which way it runs. Neither field glyph
   carries an arrow of its own, so a button has exactly one direction indicator on
   it and there is no second arrow to contradict the first.

   `sort-direction` is one half of the `sort` glyph above, moved to the centre.
   That glyph is a down arrow beside an up arrow — it opens the phone tray and so
   has to name sorting as a whole rather than any one direction — and its two
   halves are the same shape at different x. Splitting it therefore costs nothing:
   the down half drawn at x=12 is `sort-direction`, and turning that half over is
   how the up half is already drawn. So the tray's trigger and the direction on
   the buttons inside it are literally the same mark, whole and halved.

   Being one half of a two-arrow glyph is also why it is not a bare chevron. It
   keeps the shaft, which is most of the mark's weight, and that is what lets it
   hold up at the small size a button needs.

   It is drawn pointing down — a field running its ordinary way — and rotated by
   CSS when that field has been reversed, rather than drawn a second time, so the
   two directions cannot drift apart the first time either is retouched. #}
{% macro icon(name, size = 22) %}
    {# Fetch from Plex and Import from Plex are the same operation at different
       scale: one poster against a whole library. They therefore get the same
       mark, and aliasing rather than duplicating is what stops the two drifting
       apart the first time either is retouched. #}
    {% set aliases = { 'fetch': 'import' } %}
    {% set paths = {
